#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "37f0d2c0-c174-5478-a007-5fb3d32bafe2" :question/math-academy-id "e-16569" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/0f/0f5dda7937b1ffed646730040efe11464d46ef1f3ec6ae5dacf84075bd5be01b.png)\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T.$" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-y+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/c3/c3ec90119193ca9c0cb886bc7b7c698eb155e6a801fd267c0a17a0d329eff84d.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/ef/eff9e5b8ab46002bd82fa42d59f60b1bb78ca04228d7c73b52d131d222ccb894.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-1 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}y-x-2 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-y+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(2)-1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2)-1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}(2)-1-2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2}-2+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1fa3ed18-5fb9-53b2-a067-5db623a289d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16570" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/89/891b79a977ea7d9f4062d3614d7001ade71cfc10de54fcbae6608eebed7e8d1a.png)\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[ 3, \\: -1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{2}-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](images/95/95e234c5c9b32e1ea965f1cd184563699fbd2793d51a9d1e4ec96ffb4e42dc50.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+y-4 \\\\ x+{y}^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{2}+(-1)-4 \\\\ 3+(-1)^{2}-3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203827 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "5da362d4-cb58-5b51-acc0-582e3a0d42e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-16571" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n![](images/c3/c3c2b2e7a9d5b2b8f76499b0ad62d628f9d79bc7c41b6bcaf9926df4f368cdbf.png)\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f} : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](images/13/13f9eb9850462317be5a5c7da22fe53ee89b24d9ce1a9226c2d0ee1e6355ba19.png)\n\nStarting with ![](images/8b/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2xy-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}-(1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](images/d5/d5eb5a91fdd96bf6ca50539d2119a97ce9e5600b3114f708cda4d95a047e8b81.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a8100ac-e347-5b38-b6c6-bb7a2b8c0727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a8100ac-e347-5b38-b6c6-bb7a2b8c0727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291303} :answer-field/id #uuid "15c460b7-9d26-5f20-b5ee-9a0fc390ea0e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291816} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf791c87-709f-5428-b4b4-6d3c687972f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf791c87-709f-5428-b4b4-6d3c687972f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.833" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291513} :answer-field/id #uuid "57f256ed-ce3a-5b75-9072-5fb4222f2497" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39c393a2-8fbe-5fde-bcd8-175c9a07323b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c393a2-8fbe-5fde-bcd8-175c9a07323b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290871} :answer-field/id #uuid "73c83487-cc97-5bc0-935d-7dd74e2026dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292088} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db3df2f3-7adb-5a46-a733-e00d9b6d9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db3df2f3-7adb-5a46-a733-e00d9b6d9f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291548} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5d77e1-ce57-5fc9-af23-cf7376ef8646" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76b77696-fb71-5bf3-bbfc-fa9d52e3daf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-255856" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-3}} | $3$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.833$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & 2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) & 2(1) \\\\ 3(1)^{2} & 3(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-2 \\\\ (1)^{3}+(1)^{3}-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.166\\,66… \\\\ -0.166\\,66…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.833 \\\\ 0.833\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "87e64f3c-51b1-5434-ba7f-f814d56f0237" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202982}] :question/id #uuid "e2d3b902-846f-5938-a220-235340dd6162" :question/math-academy-id "q-256226" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y + x - 3 \\\\ xy^{2} - y - 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [1, 2]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$x_{2} \\approx [$$1.091$$]$\n{{field-1}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204082 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d5af280c-c756-592a-9a39-676c0b8ff81b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203013} {:answer-field/id #uuid "4f26550d-f8be-5b43-b6c1-b97ec83cf43b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203014}] :question/id #uuid "5800980a-2f76-57ac-b91f-63be30605046" :question/math-academy-id "q-256559" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{4} + 1 \\\\ x^{3}y - 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$.\n\n| $x_{1} = [$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"}]}
